# Normalize line endings for all text files
* text=auto

# Force LF line endings for specific file types.
#
# The languages this project is actually written in (.ts, .svelte, .rs) were
# missing here (the list came from the scaffold), so with core.autocrlf=true on
# a Windows clone they checked out CRLF while `biome ci` and `cargo fmt` both
# expect LF. Local checks then failed on every file regardless of its content,
# which makes them useless as a pre-push signal; CI stayed green because the
# runner checks out LF. Added 2026-07-21.
*.md text eol=lf
*.txt text eol=lf
# The name-guard cases file is generated by a Rust test and read by a Python
# control, so it wants canonical bytes in git.
#
# ⚠️ This said a CRLF checkout "would fail that test on any fresh clone -- CI
# included", and then said the test normalises CRLF two sentences later. Both
# cannot be true and it is the first that was wrong: manifest.rs compares
# `committed.replace("\r\n", "\n")`, so a CRLF checkout passes. PR #88 round
# 10, FINDING 5 -- a justification falsified by the thing it justifies.
#
# The attribute is kept on the honest reason: it keeps the committed bytes
# canonical and the diffs clean. The test's normalisation is what makes a CRLF
# checkout work, and that is where the defence actually lives.
*.tsv text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.toml text eol=lf
*.xml text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf
*.ts text eol=lf
*.svelte text eol=lf
*.rs text eol=lf
*.lock text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf

